WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.810
Hi, welcome back to our online retreat--the Wheel of Life Retreat.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:15.599
And I'd like to welcome Zhou Ping and thank her for translating today.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.100
So we're on session eight and

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.310
the name of this session is "What it's Like to Live in the Bardo."

00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:35.369
The picture we're using for this class

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:42.479
is a burnt, black tree trunk, and

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.750
we'll explain more about that later.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.919
So I think first ... most of us know the word bardo (BAR DO).

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.280
bar (BAR) means "in-between," and bardo means "the state in-between."

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.889
It means the state between after you die and until you are reborn.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.890
So we're continuing with this idea of how lousy is life, okay?

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.720
We have trouble all through our life,

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.290
and then also again in the bardo we have the same trouble.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.210
So let's talk about what it's like to be in the bardo.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:59.430
I'm going to read from the Tibetan. I hope you'll follow the Tibetan.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.968
I know not all of you want to learn Tibetan,

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:11.239
but I'm trying to put a seed in your mind

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.129
to read these very holy books.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.140
To read the original--it puts a very deep seed in your mind.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.660
And we will meet again in the next life

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:35.189
to study translation together.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.970
So I'm planting those seeds.

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:44.909
nyipa shi ne bardo drup luk ni 
(GNYIS PA SHI NAS BAR DO 'GRUB LUGS NI) 
Now the second part of this discussion is:

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.449
how is it that we go into the bardo?

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.310
And what happens in the bardo?

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.530
chi si gakpa ('CHI SRID 'GAGS PA)

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.010
chi si ('CHI SRID) is a special expression.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.029
There are four kinds of sipas (SRID PA), and

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.660
chi si is one of them.

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:17.279
In this case, si (SRID) means "kind of being."

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:29.309
And chi si means "the person or the being at the time of their death."

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:38.969
So chi si gakpa... gakpa ('GAGS PA) means "ending."

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:47.069
So chi si gakpa means "the person who is dying" on their hospital bed.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:57.989
The person is dying, and then du nyam du (DUS MNYAM DU)

00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:04.829
means "at the same moment."

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:13.229
sang dey to men shindu 
(SRANG MDA'I MTHO DMAN BZHIN DU) 
means that in the old days when they weigh food,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.810
for example, they will use a scale with two plates;

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:25.080
and as this plate comes down,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.240
this plate goes up.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.449
So they say the appearance

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.110
that you have

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.960
at the time of death--

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.830
your outer appearance--

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.440
it starts to fade,

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.779
and at the same time another appearance starts to come in,

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:58.680
and you take the appearance of the person in the bardo.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.680
So your face, your body start to change.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.100
The face and body you had when you are dying

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:22.319
is replaced by the the face and the body that you're going to have in the bardo.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.280
And that bardo person's appearance ...

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.640
here it says "ngun du kyi sipay suk chen te" 
(SNGON DUS KYI SRID PA'I GZUGS CAN TE),

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.920
it "matches the appearance of the person before."

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.080
And this is a very confusing idea,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.140
and many people misunderstood it.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.320
When I die

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:07.920
and I go to the bardo, my appearance will be the appearance "of the person before."

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:15.749
But it means the appearance of the person before your next death.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.420
So that means the appearance you are going to have in your next life.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:34.339
So, the appearance that you take on in the bardo,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.079
it's related to who you are going to be after you come out of the bardo.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.839
So here it says kyewa chimay lu kyi che yip chen du drup 
(SKYE BA PHYI MA'I LUS KYI BYAD DBYIBS CAN DU GRUB)--

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:57.290
you take on a body that is related to the body

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.290
you're going to have when you come out of the bardo.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.540
nyun du kyi sipa shepay dun 
(SNGON DUS KYI SRID PA ZHES PA'I DON) ...

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:15.109
Here he clarifies. He says, by the way, when the scripture says "the being of the time before,"

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:26.990
it means the being you're going to be for a whole lifetime before you die in the next lifetime.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:36.510
kachik gi ... (KHA CIG GIS ...

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:40.040
... shinpo de sunpay kap kyi nampa dang cha che jindra yupa shin
(... GSHIN PO DE GSON PA'I SKABS KYI RNAM PA DANG CHA BYAD JI 'DRA YOD PA BZHIN)

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.540
Some people say

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.470
the dead person

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:54.799
in the bardo, they look like the person they used to be.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:03.740
bardo la denduk pe (BAR DO LA BSDAD 'DUG PAS)--
and they sit in the bardo

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:12.619
and you can send them messages and they will reply, 
you know even with written messages to you.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:23.569
And in America, we have some fortune tellers

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:30.589
and you go to them and you say, "I want to talk to my father who died,"

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:39.529
and they kind of have trick ways to write letters and they say, 
"Oh look, he wrote you a letter."

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:55.339
sok dzun yin (ZOG RDZUN YIN)--
here says, "they're all fake." 
So he says, "be careful of these fortune tellers."

00:08:58.709 --> 00:09:00.709
Some of them are just making money.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.560
They are making fake letters from your relatives who died.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:17.510
But you can see a bardo being and you can talk to them. It's possible.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:30.950
dendra re nyi tongwa si kyang 
(DE 'DRA RE GNYIS MTHONG BA SRID KYANG) 
says "it's possible you could see

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.399
your relative in the bardo." It is possible.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:40.529
And...

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:47.299
it's also possible that someone who looks like your relative in the bardo is not really your relative in the bardo.

00:09:53.399 --> 00:09:55.260
mi mayin gyi ... (MI MA YIN GYIS ...

00:09:55.260 --> 00:10:01.459
... shinpoy suk sung ne jikten pelpa lu bar che 
(... GSHIN PO'I GZUGS BZUNG NAS 'JIG RTEN PHAL PA SLU BAR BYED)--
this is a kind of spirit being.

00:10:06.029 --> 00:10:12.588
It's a kind of ghost, and they like to take on the appearance of your relative,

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.300
and they trick you.

00:10:18.779 --> 00:10:21.648
je mi la (RJE MID LA). mi la (MIS LA) means "Milarepa,"

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.339
a very, very famous meditator from about a thousand years ago.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:35.570
And there's a story in his hundred-thousand songs,

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.729
which in Tibetan is gur bum (MGUR 'BUM), which means "hundred-thousand songs."

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:55.269
And there's a story there about how he met someone's father from the bardo.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.550
And he guided that person.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:11.229
And apparently there was also some ghost in the story who was imitating that person.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:25.029
And I'd be interested if any of you guys listening ... you could read those hundred-thousand songs of Milarepa,

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:33.279
which was translated into English a long, long time ago by somebody named Garma C.C. Chang.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:44.620
The name he translated--"hundred-thousand"--is a mistake.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.819
He made a mistranslation

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:53.500
in the title.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:12:00.250
So for example, sungbum (GSUNG 'BUM) doesn't mean a hundred-thousand books;

00:12:03.649 --> 00:12:05.649
it just means a collection of books.

00:12:08.089 --> 00:12:13.359
But generally the translation is not so bad and maybe some of you guys can find this story.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:27.499
It's not a hundred-thousand stories, but it's a lot, and I don't have time to read the whole thing, but you guys can read it.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.269
de na bardowa ni wangpo kun tsangwa 
(DES NA BAR DO BA NI DBANG PO KUN TSANG BA)
So, a bardo being

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:42.589
really does have eyes, ears, nose, and everything complete.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:56.390
ngun she yupa (MNGON SHES YOD PA) means abhijnyana--
"they have special mental abilities."

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.730
For example, they can understand all languages.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:07.550
And they have dzu trul (RDZU 'PHRUL). dzu trul means they "can do miracles."

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.380
For example, they can walk through walls,

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.869
they can travel millions of miles in an hour;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:31.280
and Buddhism says that's how new planets are populated actually. That's how we people came to this world.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:44.540
So worlds are countless, beginningless, and people move from one to another one a million miles away and they populate it.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.990
Then that ability to walk through walls,

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:09.219
it's very interesting ... if the wall is part of a temple

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.460
or a holy place, like Bodhgaya,

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:25.990
when they hit that wall, they cannot go through. 
Any wall in the world, they can walk through it, except that one.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.510
So some wall that's connected to a holy place,

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.040
it will stop them.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:45.460
And the skin at the back of a woman's womb is the same.

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:56.049
gang la-ang mi tokpa (GANG LA'ANG MI THOGS PA) 
means "they can't go through" and they get stuck there,

00:14:57.470 --> 00:15:00.399
and that's where many of them are reborn.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:08.710
Next, Pabongka Rinpoche describes the physical appearance of these beings.

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:21.609
So we said they take on the appearance that corresponds to their next birth.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:36.460
That doesn't mean if they're going to be reborn as a very tall guy that during the bardo

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:38.859
they look like a very tall guy. That's not what it means.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:50.390
It means what kind of rebirth they're going to take,

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.820
you can tell it from the appearance in the bardo.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:11.330
So he gives here some examples: nyelwa, yidak, dundro, dupay hla, mi 
(DMYAL BA, YI DVAGS, DUD 'GRO, 'DOD PA'I LHA MI). He gives five examples.

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:15.830
Person in the hell realms.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.129
Person who becomes a hungry ghost.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:23.150
Person who is going to become an animal.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.080
Person who is going to be a

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.769
pleasure being in the desire realm.

00:16:34.529 --> 00:16:39.079
And a human. What do they look like in the bardo?

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:49.609
me rim shin (MAS RIM BZHIN) means that if we start one-by-one 
to tell you from the bottom--meaning from the hell realm--

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:57.229
dong dum me tsikpa (SDONG DUM MES TSIG PA) means

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:05.689
people in the bardo who are going to be reborn into the hell realms, 
their body

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.959
looks like the "stump of a burnt tree."

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:22.910
If you're going to be born as a hungry ghost,

00:17:25.709 --> 00:17:27.709
your body looks like water.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:37.939
If you're going to be born as an animal, 
duwa (DU BA), your body looks like "smoke."

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:46.970
dupay lha, mi--if you're going to be a pleasure being 
or human in the desire realm,

00:17:52.680 --> 00:18:00.289
ser sangpo tabuy dok tar 
(GSER BZANG PO LTA BU'I MDOG LTAR)--
you have a "body that looks like very fine gold."

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:12.560
suk kampay bardo karpor yu la 
(GZUGS KHAMS PA'I BAR DO DKAR POR YOD LA)--
if you are going to be born into the form realm 
(maybe you did those four meditations, right?),

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:22.639
your "body will be like a shining white color

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.290
in the bardo."

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:34.399
suk me du kye na bardo me 
(GZUGS MED DU SKYE NA BAR DO MED), 
but "if you're going to be born into the formless realm

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:39.290
there is no bardo,"

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:43.190
because you're not going to have a body.

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:49.219
So it's interesting, if I die here, sitting here,

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:56.900
I will go immediately into my mental body.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:08.000
Right here. And that's where the formless realm is--wherever I die.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:17.660
But otherwise, kind of the purpose of the bardo

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:25.400
is to travel from the place you die to the place you're going to be born.

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.730
It could be one meter.

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:34.490
It could be millions of miles.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:37.879
And it takes the same time.

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:44.759
Okay, the next paragraph says: 
me gewa chepay bar si ni
(MI DGE BA BYED PA'I BAR SRID NI).

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:55.619
Again, si means "the person," bar means "in the bardo"--
so bar si means "the person in the bardo."

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:05.579
If, generally speaking, they were not a very nice person 
their whole life, before,

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:16.470
tsen mo mun nak tu nangwa tabu 
(MTSAN MO MUN NAG TU SNANG BA LTA BU)--
"they look kind of like

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:20.490
darkness."

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:25.770
And that's their appearance. They look like dark, black.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:36.120
Like night.  
gewa chepay bar si ni 
(DGE BA BYED PA'I BAR SRID NI)--
But the "bardo of a person who was a good person their whole life,"

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.069
nambu karpo (SNAM BU DKAR PO) means they look like "white silk."

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:51.480
da u dang chepay tsen moy nangwa
(ZLA 'OD DANG BCAS PA'I MTSAN MO'I SNANG BA)--
they "look like the

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:54.730
white

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:56.730
moonlight at night."

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:04.499
bardoway tse tse (BAR DO BA'I TSE TSAD) means 
"how long do these beings live for?"

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:13.560
sung tsul mang yang (GSUNGS TSUL MANG YANG)--
There's "many, many different scriptures that say different things."

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:20.070
Most of those scriptures are incorrect.

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:28.590
There's one in particular called ... they translated it as "Tibetan Book of the Dead."

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:39.720
bardo trang drul (BAR DO 'PHRANG SGROL), and in the tradition of Tsongkhapa, 
who is very, very strict about his authoritative sources,

00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:50.679
he says the original of that book

00:21:53.179 --> 00:21:58.539
is this word here ... it's ... fake. It's fake--

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:00.650
so dzun (BZO RDZUN).

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:02.650
It's just not correct.

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:09.040
So this tradition--Tsongkapa's tradition--would say

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:12.100
that book is not correct about the bardo.

00:22:17.809 --> 00:22:21.789
pel cher shak dun du shepa 
(PHAL CHER ZHAG BDUN DU BSHAD PA)--
but most of the great books

00:22:24.679 --> 00:22:32.079
chimay tsoy shak in dra 
(PHYI MA'I TSO'I ZHAG YIN 'DRA)--
say that bardo being always lives for seven days.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:37.570
They cannot live for more than seven days.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:48.099
Then there is a funny debate where Pabongka says, 
"I wonder which kind of day they're talking about."

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:00.579
Because a day in the hells or a day

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:05.709
for the pleasure beings can be a thousand years ... of human years.

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:21.939
And he says chimay tsoy shak (PHYI MA'I TSO'I ZHAG)--
shak (ZHAG) means "one day and night" or 24 hours.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:25.450
yin dra (YIN 'DRA) means "my opinion,"

00:23:28.309 --> 00:23:31.419
it's seven days of where you're going to.

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:38.199
But he's not totally sure. 
yin dra means "I'm not totally sure."

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:47.319
But traditionally, in the Buddhist monastery for example,

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:56.330
we count seven days; and we start from the day that the person died.

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:03.379
So if they died at one minute to midnight on Tuesday,

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:14.299
one minute before midnight on Tuesday night, they die at 11:59 at night,

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:25.219
still we count that as number one when we count seven days.

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:37.340
And I had an opportunity on a long plane flight once, 
I talked to my own teacher--

00:24:42.059 --> 00:24:48.499
who again was a disciple of Pabongka Rinpoche and 
Pabongka Rinpoche's heart disciple, Trijang Rinpoche--

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:58.609
and I asked him many questions about these bardos.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.530
And he gave me some great answers and

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:10.460
I put all of those into a question-and-answer paper,

00:25:14.789 --> 00:25:17.658
and we're going to offer it on this broadcast.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:27.949
There'll be a link that you can click on, and 
you can go to that explanation by my teacher.

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:37.220
shak dun ne chi (ZHAG BDUN NAS 'CHI)--then they will "die after seven days."

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:47.480
Then either they go to the life they're supposed to go to, 
or they come back to another bardo for seven more days.

00:25:53.859 --> 00:26:00.129
Or less. So let's say they're in the bardo for ten days,

00:26:02.690 --> 00:26:05.649
after the first seven days they will die,

00:26:08.239 --> 00:26:10.239
they will come back to that bardo

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:16.840
for three more days. Then they die and become whatever they're supposed to become.

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:24.200
So he calls it here, he has a funny name, 
he calls it chi chung re ('CHI CHUNG RE),
he means "mini-death." Mini-death.

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:31.100
They have bardo mini-deaths

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:34.759
every seven days.

00:26:34.759 --> 00:26:38.169
kye ne ma nye tse (SKYE GNAS MA RNYED TSE)--
and "if they don't find

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:41.289
the rebirth" that they are supposed to have,

00:26:45.379 --> 00:26:50.079
len dun gyi bar detar chi chung re che sung
(LAN BDUN GYI BAR DE LTAR 'CHI CHUNG RE BYED GSUNGS)--
the great books say,

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:57.099
and for example the Abhidharma Kosha, which is one of the greatest books of Buddhism ...

00:27:01.219 --> 00:27:08.619
and if you're really interested about the bardo, you should read Stanley's translation of the Abhidharma

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:14.349
and that's completely correct.

00:27:16.849 --> 00:27:19.119
It's almost two thousand years ago.

00:27:20.839 --> 00:27:28.449
And that's a better source to read than the "Tibetan Book of the Dead," 
which has many false views in it.

00:27:36.529 --> 00:27:43.809
len dun gyi bar detar chi chung re che sung--
"those great books say you will have maximum seven mini-deaths."

00:27:53.179 --> 00:27:56.949
So you must take your rebirth within 49 days,

00:28:00.919 --> 00:28:03.488
counting the day of the death as the first day.

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:11.619
That's why we traditionally do those jang chok (BYANG CHOG) prayers,

00:28:16.369 --> 00:28:19.419
reminding grandpa of all the good things he did.

00:28:23.929 --> 00:28:28.658
Traditionally, we do those prayers on the seventh day after death--counting the death day as the first day.

00:28:33.919 --> 00:28:37.088
And then again on the 49th day,

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:42.069
because within that time period

00:28:44.239 --> 00:28:46.929
they must go on to their next life.

00:28:47.929 --> 00:28:53.258
And the seed must be activated for that next life.

00:28:55.339 --> 00:28:58.208
Sorry, not activated--it must open for the next life.

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:07.570
It's activated as they die from the past life ...

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:14.469
their last awareness of that life,

00:29:17.899 --> 00:29:21.429
and then it actually opens at the end of the bardo

00:29:24.619 --> 00:29:27.039
and they are born into their next life.

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.490
And now ...

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:38.810
we didn't learn these things in school or in university.  
Princeton doesn't have a course on bardo.

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:47.780
But everyone has to die,

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:51.740
everyone goes through this bardo,

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:55.489
and it's important to study it, and

00:29:57.030 --> 00:29:59.030
it's important to learn it.

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:02.420
There's a joke in the monastery:

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:10.460
If you know you're going on a ... 
like I used to go there to debate in the geshe (DGE BSHES) courses,

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:15.200
and I

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:21.320
remember on the day I was coming back to America, 
I would debate in the morning, and then I would run to the airport

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:23.160
and come back.

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:29.540
On the day I had to leave the monastery to come back to America, 
I would still debate in the morning.

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:42.080
And I remember the monks used to ask me, 
"You're going to leave today--why are you debating in the morning?"

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:48.050
"Just go get your suitcase ready."

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:51.920
So we're all going to die.

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:56.359
It might be today. Get your suitcase ready!

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:00.770
Study the bardo.

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:07.129
We don't know if we're going to enjoy the money or the family we have next week.

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:09.979
We don't know if we will still be here.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:19.010
But sooner or later we have to go to the bardo,

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:23.300
so let's pack our suitcase,

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:26.830
let's get ready for that, okay?

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:31.660
See you in the next online session, which is number nine.

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:36.250
And thank you for translating, Zhou Ping.

